#
# FILE            feature_builds.test
#
# AUTHOR          Ken Zangelin
#
# Copyright 2026 Seamware
#
# Does a reduced build still COMPILE, LINK, and START?
#
# The rest of the suite cannot answer that. It runs against one installed
# binary, so it tests the configuration that binary was built with and no
# other - and the failures a reduced build produces are mostly upstream of any
# request. A missing service routine is a link error; a plugin whose driver
# table still names a function its own CMakeLists excluded LINKS FINE and dies
# at dlopen, which is how both of the first two features broke. Neither reaches
# a test that needs a running broker, because there is no running broker.
#
# So this case builds the broker itself, several times, and asks each result to
# start and describe itself. It is the cheapest possible check and it catches
# the whole class.
#
# ⚠️ Minutes, not seconds - five cmake builds. REQUIRE_BUILDTESTS keeps it out
# of the run set unless `corTest -buildTests yes` asks for it, which is a
# separate CI job.
#
# It expects a normal build to have happened first: cmake needs the generated
# coraineStack.h and coraineBuild.h, which `make di` writes into the source
# tree. Running the suite at all already implies that.
#
# Only the configurations that DO something are covered. Nine of the fifteen
# features are declared and unread, so a case for one would build a
# byte-identical binary and assert nothing, and three do not link - both states
# are documented in doc/building.md rather than pinned here, because a test
# that goes red when someone IMPLEMENTS a feature is a trap, not a guard.
#
# REQUIRE_BUILDTESTS: yes
#
--NAME--
Reduced builds compile, link and start — and report what they are

--TAGS--
build features slow

--INIT--

--RUN--

echo "01. Build the broker in each reduced configuration and start it"
echo "==============================================================="
test/funcTests/tools/featureBuildCheck.sh
echo
echo


--EXPECT--
01. Build the broker in each reduced configuration and start it
===============================================================
REGISTRATIONS=OFF: build ok, start ok, REGISTRATIONS=false SUBSCRIPTIONS=true
SUBSCRIPTIONS=OFF: build ok, start ok, REGISTRATIONS=true SUBSCRIPTIONS=false
REGISTRATIONS+SUBSCRIPTIONS=OFF: build ok, start ok, REGISTRATIONS=false SUBSCRIPTIONS=false
MONGOC=OFF: build ok, start ok, REGISTRATIONS=true SUBSCRIPTIONS=true
ADMIN_API=OFF: build ok, start ok, REGISTRATIONS=true SUBSCRIPTIONS=true


--TEARDOWN--
# The build dirs are big and the brokers are started by the script rather than
# by coraineStart, so neither is the harness's to clean up. The pid file is what
# makes this decidable from observable state - a teardown runs as its own
# process and knows nothing of what --RUN-- did.
if [ -f /tmp/coraine-featureBuild/pids ]; then
  while read -r p; do kill "$p" 2>/dev/null; done < /tmp/coraine-featureBuild/pids
fi
rm -rf /tmp/coraine-featureBuild
